k43 implements Regex::exec as:
  sub exec {
    my ( $self, $str ) = @_;
    $str = defined($str) ? "$str" : '';
    my @captures = $str =~ $self->_qr;
    return undef unless @captures;
    return [ $&, @captures ];
  }

In list context, $str =~ PATTERN returns:
  - the captured groups if any groups exist;
  - the boolean match result (builtin::true / builtin::false) if no groups exist.

Measured on Perl 5.40:
  my @r = "xx" =~ /x/;       # r == (builtin::true)  -- the boolean
  my @r = "xy" =~ /(.)/;     # r == ("x")             -- the capture

So for a pattern with no capture groups, the port returns:
  [$&, (builtin::true_or_false)]

which becomes [full_match, true/false], or via _js_to_string "[full,true]".

JS RegExp.prototype.exec returns [full_match, ...captures] -- no boolean -- for any pattern.

Probe (Perl port):
  {% set r = r/x/g %}{% set m = r.exec("xx") %}{{ m }}   => "x,true"

Oracle for the same case is unmeasured because the corpus has no no-capture-group exec case (the corpus exercises `r/(.)(.)/.exec("ab")` which has 2 groups and incidentally matches). The latent divergence surfaces only when a template does {{ r/x/.exec("xy") }} or iterates the result.

Required fix: detect "no capture groups" in exec and drop the boolean. Easy proxy: check if the pattern body has unescaped "(" not followed by "?:" / "?=" / etc; or compile the qr with a sentinel and inspect $1..$n.

Add a corpus case pinning the no-groups behaviour (e.g. {{ r/x/.exec("xx") }} -> "x") and at least one keyed access (m[0] and m[1]) to lock the shape.

